INSERT INTO `lecture_notes` VALUES (1,1,'This lecture delivers a structured breakdown of Arrays: In-Depth Master Lecture. It develops an intuition for why these concepts exist, how they are represented in modern computer architecture, and how engineers evaluate performance.','1. Formal Definition and Scope\n2. Underlying Data Representations & Invariants\n3. Operational Complexity (Worst, Best, Average Case)\n4. Memory Allocation and Hardware Trade-offs\n5. Practical Engineering Edge Cases','ÔÇó Always establish base invariants before executing transformations.\nÔÇó Watch for null references, pointer swings, and boundary overflows.\nÔÇó Balance asymptotic efficiency against practical cache locality.\nÔÇó Understand trade-offs between time complexity and space overhead.','Quick Revision:\n- Review operational diagrams and node pointer changes.\n- Recall standard complexity bounds: O(1), O(log n), O(n), and O(n log n).\n- Practice walking through algorithms step-by-step with pen and paper.','Q1: Explain the primary architectural trade-offs of this approach.\nQ2: Derive the time and space complexity under worst-case inputs.\nQ3: How does this mechanism handle concurrency or boundary exceptions?\nQ4: Compare and contrast this approach with alternative data structures.','2026-09-01 07:26:29','2026-09-01 07:26:29'),(2,2,'This lecture delivers a structured breakdown of Linked Lists: In-Depth Master Lecture. It develops an intuition for why these concepts exist, how they are represented in modern computer architecture, and how engineers evaluate performance.','1. Formal Definition and Scope\n2. Underlying Data Representations & Invariants\n3. Operational Complexity (Worst, Best, Average Case)\n4. Memory Allocation and Hardware Trade-offs\n5. Practical Engineering Edge Cases','ÔÇó Always establish base invariants before executing transformations.\nÔÇó Watch for null references, pointer swings, and boundary overflows.\nÔÇó Balance asymptotic efficiency against practical cache