','1. Understand the core principles of Database Systems Significance and Advantages\n2. Know the time and space complexity of common operations\n3. Be able to implement basic operations from scratch\n4. Understand when to use Database Systems Significance and Advantages vs other approaches\n5. Practice with real-world examples and edge cases','Revision Notes for Database Systems Significance and Advantages:\n\n• Definition: Database Systems Significance and Advantages is a fundamental concept in computer science\n• Key Properties: Understanding the defining characteristics\n• Operations: Learn the basic and advanced operations\n• Complexity: Know the time and space complexity\n• Applications: Real-world use cases\n• Common Mistakes: Avoid these common pitfalls','1. What is Database Systems Significance and Advantages and why is it important?\n2. Explain the basic operations of Database Systems Significance and Advantages with examples\n3. What is the time complexity of key operations?\n4. Compare Database Systems Significance and Advantages with alternative approaches\n5. Implement a basic example of Database Systems Significance and Advantages from scratch','2026-09-03 11:29:57','2026-09-03 11:29:57'),(74,108,'This lecture provides a comprehensive introduction to Basic Concepts of Indexing and Ordered Indices in the context of Database Management Systems. Students will learn the fundamental principles, key concepts, and practical applications of Basic Concepts of Indexing and Ordered Indices.','Basic Concepts of Indexing and Ordered Indices Fundamentals, Core Concepts, Implementation, Applications, Common Patterns, Best Practices','1. Understand the core principles of Basic Concepts of Indexing and Ordered Indices\n2. Know the time and space complexity of common operations\n3. Be able to implement basic operations from scratch\n4. Understand when to use Basic Concepts of Indexing and Ordered Indices vs other approaches\n5. Practice with real-world examples and edge cases','Revision Notes for Basic Concepts of Indexing and Ordered Indices:\n\n• Definition: Basic Concepts of Indexing and Ordered Indices is a fundamental concept in computer science\n• Key Properties: Understanding the defining characteristics\n• Operations: Learn the basic and advanced operations\n• Complexity: Know the time and space complexity\n• Applications: Real-world use cases\n• Common Mistakes: Avoid these common pitfalls','1. What is Basic Concepts of Indexing and Ordered Indices and why is it important?\n2. Explain the basic operations of Basic Concepts of Indexing and Ordered Indices with examples\n3. What is the time complexity of key operations?\n4. Compare Basic Concepts of Indexing and Ordered Indices with alternative approaches\n5. Implement a basic example of Basic Concepts of Indexing and Ordered Indices from scratch','2026-09-03 11:30:00','2026-09-03 11:30:00'),(75,110,'This lecture provides a comprehensive introduction to Denormalization and Database Design in the context of Database Management Systems. Students will learn the fundamental principles, key concepts, and practical applications of Denormalization and Database Design.','Denormalization and Database Design Fundamentals, Core Concepts, Implementation, Applications, Common Patterns, Best Practices','1. Understand the core principles of Denormalization and Database Design\n2. Know the time and space complexity of common operations\n3. Be able to implement basic operations from scratch\n4. Understand when to use Denormalization and Database Design vs other approaches\n5. Practice with real-world examples and edge cases','Revision Notes for Denormalization and Database Design:\n\n• Definition: Denormalization and Database Design is a fundamental concept in computer science\n• Key Properties: Understanding the defining characteristics\n• Operations: Learn the basic and advanced operations\n• Complexity: Know the time and space complexity\n• Applications: Real-world use cases\n• Common Mistakes: Avoid these common pitfalls','1. What is Denormalization and Database Design and why is it important?\n2. Explain the basic operations of Denormalization and Database Design with examples\n3. What is the time complexity of key operations?\n4. Compare Denormalization and Database Design with alternative approaches\n5. Implement a basic example of Denormalization and Database Design from scratch','2026-09-03 11:30:05','2026-09-03 11:30:05'),(76,112,'This lecture provides a comprehensive introduction to Deadlock Handling and Time-stamp Based Protocols in the context of Database Management Systems. Students will learn the fundamental principles, key concepts, and practical applications of Deadlock Handling and Time-stamp Based Protocols.','Deadlock Handling and Time-stamp Based Protocols Fundamentals, Core Concepts, Implementation, Applications, Common Patterns, Best Practices','1. Understand the core principles of Deadlock Handling and Time-stamp Based Protocols\n2. Know the time and space complexity of common operations\n3. Be able to implement basic operations from scratch\n4. Understand when to use Deadlock Handling and Time-stamp Based Protocols vs other approaches\n5. Practice with real-world examples and edge cases','Revision Notes for Deadlock Handling and Time-stamp Based Protocols:\n\n• Definition: Deadlock Handling and Time-stamp Based Protocols is a fundamental concept in computer science\n• Key Properties: Understanding the defining characteristics\n• Operations: Learn the basic and advanced operations\n• Complexity: Know the time and space complexity\n• Applications: Real-world use cases\n• Common Mistakes: Avoid these common pitfalls','1. What is Deadlock Handling and Time-stamp Based Protocols and why is it important?\n2. Explain the basic operations of Deadlock Handling and Time-stamp Based Protocols with examples\n3. What is the time complexity of key operations?\n4. Compare Deadlock Handling and Time-stamp Based Protocols with alternative approaches\n5. Implement a basic example of Deadlock Handling and Time-stamp Based Protocols from scratch','2026-09-03 11:30:09','2026-09-03 11:30:09'),(77,114,'This lecture provides a comprehensive introduction to Backtracking: 8-Queen Problem in the context of Database Management Systems. Students will learn the fundamental principles, key concepts, and practical applications of Backtracking: 8-Queen Problem.','Backtracking: 8-Queen Problem Fundamentals, Core Concepts, Implementation, Applications, Common Patterns, Best Practices','1. Understand the core principles of Backtracking: 8-Queen Problem\n2. Know the time and space complexity of common operations\n3. Be able to implement basic operations from scratch\n4. Understand when to use Backtracking: 8-Queen Problem vs other approaches\n5. Practice with real-world examples and edge cases','Revision Notes for Backtracking: 8-Queen Problem:\n\n• Definition: Backtracking: 8-Queen Problem is a fundamental concept in computer science\n• Key Properties: Understanding the defining characteristics\n• Operations: Learn the basic and advanced operations\n• Complexity: Know the time and space complexity\n• Applications: Real-world use cases\n• Common Mistakes: Avoid these common pitfalls','1. What is Backtracking: 8-Queen Problem and why is it important?\n2. Explain the basic operations of Backtracking: 8-Queen Problem with examples\n3. What is the time complexity of key operations?\n4. Compare Backtracking: 8-Queen Problem with alternative approaches\n5. Implement a basic example of Backtracking: 8-Queen Problem from scratch','2026-09-03 11:30:13','2026-09-03 11:30:13'),(78,116,'This lecture provides a comprehensive introduction to Authorization and Advanced SQL in the context of Database Management Systems. Students will learn the fundamental principles, key concepts, and practical applications of Authorization and Advanced SQL.','SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, WHERE, JOIN, GROUP BY, Aggregate Functions','1. Understand the core principles of Authorization and Advanced SQL\n2. Know the time and space complexity of common operations\n3. Be able to implement basic operations from scratch\n4. Understand when to use Authorization and Advanced SQL vs other approaches\n5. Practice with real-world examples and edge cases','Revision Notes for Authorization and Advanced SQL:\n\n• Definition: Authorization and Advanced SQL is a fundamental concept in computer science\n• Key Properties: Understanding the defining characteristics\n• Operations: Learn the basic and advanced operations\n• Complexity: Know the time and space complexity\n• Applications: Real-world use cases\n• Common Mistakes: Avoid these common pitfalls','1. What is Authorization and Advanced SQL and why is it important?\n2. Explain the basic operations of Authorization and Advanced SQL with examples\n3. What is the time complexity of key operations?\n4. Compare Authorization and Advanced SQL with alternative approaches\n5. Implement a basic example of Authorization and Advanced SQL from scratch','2026-09-03 11:30:18','2026-09-03 11:30:18'),(108,176,'This module introduces compiler architecture, translation phases, regular language tokenization, and deterministic top-down grammar parsing.','• Compiler vs Interpreter Differences\n• Six Major Compilation Phases (Lexical, Syntax, Semantic, Intermediate Code, Optimization, Code Generation)\n• Regular Expressions to Finite Automata (Thompson Construction & Subset Construction)\n• Context-Free Grammars (CFG) and Parse Trees\n• Elimination of Left Recursion and Left Factoring for LL(1) Parsing','• Lexical analysis produces a stream of tokens and constructs the initial symbol table entries.\n• Top-down predictive parsers cannot process left-recursive grammars without infinite recursion.\n• Bootstrapping allows a compiler to be written in its own source language once a base core exists.','1. Convert CFG to LL(1) by removing left recursion: A -> Aa | b becomes A -> bA\', A\' -> aA\' | epsilon.\n2. Left factoring eliminates common prefixes: A -> ab1 | ab2 becomes A -> aA\', A\' -> b1 | b2.\n3. Verify LL(1) condition: FIRST and FOLLOW sets for disjoint alternative productions.','Q1: Explain the six phases of a compiler with a neat translation diagram for position = initial + rate * 60.\nQ2: How do you eliminate immediate left recursion from a context-free grammar?\nQ3: Construct the predictive parsing table for an LL(1) arithmetic grammar and verify parse steps.\nQ4: Differentiate between a compiler pass and a compiler phase.','2026-09-03 11:37:19','2026-09-03 11:37:19'),(109,177,'Detailed exploration of shift-reduce parsing, handles, handle pruning, and the mathematical construction of SLR, CLR, and LALR parsing tables.','• Handles and Handle Pruning in Rightmost Derivations in Reverse\n• Shift-Reduce Stack Operations (Shift, Reduce, Accept, Error)\n• Parsing Conflicts: Shift-Reduce (S/R) and Reduce-Reduce (R/R)\n• LR(0), SLR(1), CLR(1), and LALR(1) Parsing Table Construction\n• Automatic Parser Generation using YACC','• CLR(1) is the most powerful deterministic LR parser but requires an enormous number of states.\n• LALR(1) merges states with the same LR(0) core, preventing shift-reduce conflicts while occasionally introducing reduce-reduce conflicts.\n• Shift-reduce conflicts occur when the parser cannot decide whether to shift the next token or reduce the current handle.','1. Handle: substring that matches RHS of production and whose reduction corresponds to a step in reverse rightmost derivation.\n2. SLR(1) reduce condition: Place r_j in state i for column a if A -> alpha. is in state i and a is in FOLLOW(A).\n3. LALR(1) table size equals SLR(1) table size, with the parsing power of CLR(1).','Q1: Trace the shift-reduce parsing actions on stack for the input id + id * id.\nQ2: What is a handle? Explain handle pruning with a concrete grammar derivation.\nQ3: Construct the Canonical Collection of LR(0) items and build the SLR(1) parsing table.\nQ4: Differentiate between CLR(1) and LALR(1) parsing algorithms regarding state count and conflict possibilities.','2026-09-03 11:37:20','2026-09-03 11:37:20'),(110,178,'Covers semantic rules, attribute grammars (S-attributed vs L-attributed), and converting language statements into Three-Address Code representations.','• Synthesized vs Inherited Attributes in SDDs\n• S-Attributed Definitions and Bottom-Up Evaluation on Parser Stacks\n• L-Attributed Definitions and Top-Down Depth-First Traversal\n• Three-Address Code (TAC) Structure (Quadruples, Triples, Indirect Triples)\n• Backpatching and Boolean Control-Flow Expression Translation','• Intermediate code separates front-end language parsing from back-end machine-specific hardware generation.\n• Quadruples contain 4 fields: op, arg1, arg2, result.\n• Triples eliminate temporary variables by referring to instructions by position index.','1. S-attributed: synthesized only; evaluated bottom-up with Postfix SDT.\n2. L-attributed: synthesized or inherited from left siblings/parent; evaluated via depth-first traversal.\n3. TAC Quadruple representation: (op, arg1, arg2, result).','Q1: Differentiate between synthesized and inherited attributes with examples.\nQ2: Generate three-address code, quadruples, and triples for: x = a + b * c - d / e.\nQ3: Explain backpatching in Boolean flow-of-control translation with a code snippet.\nQ4: Construct a syntax tree and DAG for the arithmetic statement a = b * -c + b * -c.','2026-09-03 11:37:22','2026-09-03 11:37:22'),(111,179,'Explores static and dynamic type systems, runtime memory partitions, activation records, parameter passing, and non-local variable access.','• Static vs Dynamic Type Checking\n• Type Expressions and Structural vs Name Type Equivalence\n• Runtime Memory Organization: Code Segment, Static Area, Stack, Heap\n• Anatomy of an Activation Record (Frame Pointer, Return Address, Locals, Temporaries)\n• Access Links and Displays for Non-Local Variable References','• Stack allocation supports recursion because each invocation receives its own dedicated activation record.\n• Dynamic memory allocation requires heap management with garbage collection or manual deallocation.\n• Symbol tables persist variable types, memory offsets, and scoping scopes across compiler phases.','1. Activation record fields: Temporary values, local variables, saved machine status, access link, control link, return value, actual parameters.\n2. Access link (static link): points to activation record of immediate lexical enclosing procedure.\n3. Control link (dynamic link): points to activation record of calling procedure.','Q1: Draw and explain the complete structure of a function activation record.\nQ2: Differentiate between static and dynamic scoping with execution traces.\nQ3: How do access links and displays resolve non-local variable lookups in nested procedures?\nQ4: Explain parameter passing mechanisms: Call by Value, Call by Reference, and Call by Name.','2026-09-03 11:37:23','2026-09-03 11:37:23'),(112,180,'Covers basic block partitioning, Control Flow Graphs, DAG-based local optimization, loop optimizations, and global data flow analysis.','• Basic Block Partitioning (Leaders: first statement, target of jump, statement following jump)\n• Control Flow Graph (CFG) Construction\n• Local Optimization via DAG Representation\n• Principal Sources of Optimization: Common Subexpression Elimination, Copy Propagation, Dead Code Elimination, Loop Invariant Motion, Strength Reduction\n• Global Data Flow Frameworks (IN/OUT equations, Gen and Kill sets)','• Optimization must preserve program semantics; it must never alter the observable output of the program.\n• Loop optimization yields the highest execution performance gains since programs spend most time in loops.\n• DAG interior nodes represent operators; leaf nodes represent initial values of variables.','1. Finding leaders: (a) Statement 1 is a leader, (b) Target of conditional/unconditional jump is a leader, (c) Statement immediately following jump is a leader.\n2. Strength reduction: replacing expensive operations (x * 8) with cheaper ones (x << 3).\n3. Reaching definitions: IN[B] = Union(OUT[P]), OUT[B] = Gen[B] U (IN[B] - Kill[B]).','Q1: Write the algorithm to partition three-address code into basic blocks and identify leaders.\nQ2: Construct the DAG for the basic block: d = b * c; e = a + b; b = b * c; a = e - d.\nQ3: Explain Loop Invariant Computation and Strength Reduction with code examples.\nQ4: Formulate the data flow equations for Live Variable Analysis.','2026-09-03 11:37:25','2026-09-03 11:37:25'),(113,181,'Introduces artificial intelligence paradigms, intelligent agent models, PEAS environment specifications, and state-space problem formulation.','• Definition and Rationality in AI\n• PEAS Specification (Performance, Environment, Actuators, Sensors)\n• Environment Properties: Fully vs Partially Observable, Deterministic vs Stochastic, Episodic vs Sequential, Static vs Dynamic, Discrete vs Continuous\n• Four Intelligent Agent Architectures: Simple Reflex, Model-Based, Goal-Based, Utility-Based\n• Problem Formulation: State Space, Initial State, Actions, Transition Model, Goal Test, Path Cost','• Rationality depends on the performance measure, prior knowledge, available actions, and percept sequence—not on omniscience.\n• A utility-based agent provides a continuous measure of performance to trade off competing goals under risk.\n• Formulating problems in state space is the prerequisite for all heuristic search algorithms.','1. PEAS framework: specify P, E, A, S for automated taxi driver, medical diagnosis system, vacuum cleaner.\n2. Agent Function maps percept sequences to actions: f: P* -> A.\n3. State space abstraction eliminates irrelevant real-world details.','Q1: Define an intelligent agent. Specify PEAS for an automated vacuum cleaner and an automated taxi.\nQ2: Explain the four primary agent architectures with block diagrams.\nQ3: What makes an environment dynamic and partially observable? Give two examples.\nQ4: Formulate the 8-puzzle problem and the Vacuum World problem as state-space search problems.','2026-09-03 11:37:26','2026-09-03 11:37:26'),(114,182,'Covers uninformed and informed search, A* admissibility, CSP constraint propagation, and two-player zero-sum Minimax with Alpha-Beta pruning.','• Uninformed Search: BFS (optimal for unit cost), DFS, Uniform-Cost Search (Dijkstra variant)\n• A* Search: f(n) = g(n) + h(n), Admissibility condition: h(n) <= h*(n), Consistency: h(n) <= c(n,a,n\') + h(n\')\n• Local Search: Hill Climbing, Local Maxima, Simulated Annealing with Boltzmann probability\n• Constraint Satisfaction Problems: Variables, Domains, Constraints, Arc Consistency (AC-3)\n• Adversarial Search: Minimax Value, Alpha-Beta Pruning conditions (alpha >= beta)','• A* tree search requires admissibility for optimality; A* graph search requires consistency to prevent revisiting closed states.\n• Alpha-Beta pruning does not change the minimax value of the root; it only skips exploration of subtrees that cannot influence the decision.\n• AC-3 enforces that every value in variable Xi has an allowed partner in variable Xj.','1. A* evaluation function: f(n) = g(n) + h(n).\n2. Admissible heuristic: h(n) never overestimates actual cost to goal.\n3. Alpha cut-off: when beta <= alpha at a MIN node, prune remaining children.','Q1: Prove that A* graph search is optimal when the heuristic function h(n) is consistent.\nQ2: Apply Alpha-Beta pruning on a given game tree of depth 3 and identify pruned nodes.\nQ3: Solve the 4-Queens problem using Backtracking Search combined with Forward Checking.\nQ4: Explain the working of Simulated Annealing and how it escapes local extrema.','2026-09-03 11:37:27','2026-09-03 11:37:27'),(115,183,'Covers First Order Logic, Unification, resolution refutation proofs in CNF, forward/backward chaining, and ontological categories.','• First Order Predicate Logic: Syntax, Semantics, Quantifiers (Universal, Existential)\n• Unification Algorithm and Most General Unifier (MGU)\n• Conversion to Conjunctive Normal Form (CNF): Skolemization, Prenex form, Elimination of implications\n• Resolution Refutation Proofs (Deriving the Empty Clause / Contradiction)\n• Forward Chaining vs Backward Chaining in Rule-Based Expert Systems','• Skolemization replaces existentially quantified variables with Skolem constants or Skolem functions of universally quantified outer variables.\n• Resolution is refutation-complete: if a set of sentences is unsatisfiable, resolution will always derive a contradiction.\n• Prolog evaluates Horn clauses via depth-first backward chaining with backtracking.','1. CNF conversion steps: Eliminate <=>, Eliminate =>, Move ~ inwards, Standardize variables, Skolemize, Drop universals, Distribute OR over AND.\n2. Resolution rule: (A v B) and (~B v C) yields resolvent (A v C).\n3. Most General Unifier: unifies two literals with the minimal variable substitution.','Q1: Convert the following English sentences into First Order Predicate Logic: \"Every student who studies passes\".\nQ2: Explain Skolemization with examples involving universal and existential quantifiers.\nQ3: Prove by Resolution Refutation in CNF that: \"Marcus is dead\" given a set of premise axioms.\nQ4: Differentiate between Forward Chaining and Backward Chaining inference mechanisms.','2026-09-03 11:37:29','2026-09-03 11:37:29'),(116,184,'Explores multi-agent coordination, agent communication languages (FIPA-ACL), negotiation bargaining protocols, and reputation models.','• Autonomous Agent Characteristics: Autonomy, Reactivity, Proactiveness, Social Ability\n• Agent Communication: Speech Act Theory, KQML (Performatives), FIPA-ACL Standard\n• Coordination and Task Allocation: Contract Net Protocol (Manager Call for Proposals, Bids, Award)\n• Negotiation and Bargaining: Alternating Offers, Pareto Efficiency, Argumentation Frameworks\n• Trust and Reputation Models (Direct observation vs peer recommendations)','• In FIPA-ACL, performatives like inform, request, propose, and accept convey intentional meaning.\n• Contract Net Protocol allocates tasks dynamically without requiring centralized global knowledge.\n• Reputation models aggregate historical interaction ratings to establish probabilistic reliability scores.','1. Contract Net Protocol steps: Announcement -> Bidding -> Awarding -> Execution -> Result.\n2. Performative: message verb specifying illocutionary force (e.g. REQUEST, INFORM).\n3. Trust: subjective expectation of another agent\'s future cooperative performance.','Q1: Explain the architectural phases of the Contract Net Protocol for distributed task sharing.\nQ2: Describe the anatomy of a FIPA-ACL message with its mandatory header parameters.\nQ3: How does game-theoretic negotiation differ from argumentation-based negotiation among agents?\nQ4: Describe how trust and reputation systems isolate Byzantine agents in peer-to-peer multi-agent networks.','2026-09-03 11:37:30','2026-09-03 11:37:30'),(117,185,'Covers probabilistic reasoning under uncertainty, Bayes theorem, Supervised vs Unsupervised vs RL, SVMs, and Decision Tree ensembles.','• Reasoning Under Uncertainty: Prior and Conditional Probability, Bayes Rule, Independence\n• Machine Learning Taxonomy: Supervised, Unsupervised, Reinforcement Learning\n• Linear Regression and Mean Squared Error Loss Function\n• Support Vector Machines (SVM): Maximum Margin Hyperplane, Support Vectors, Kernel Trick\n• Decision Trees (Entropy, Information Gain, ID3/C4.5) and Random Forest Bagging','• Bayes Rule: P(A|B) = [P(B|A) * P(A)] / P(B) enables updating prior beliefs with diagnostic evidence.\n• SVM finds the optimal hyperplane that maximizes the geometric margin between data classes.\n• Random Forests ensemble multiple de-correlated decision trees to reduce variance and mitigate overfitting.','1. Entropy H(S) = - sum(p_i * log2(p_i)).\n2. Information Gain = H(S) - sum(|Sv|/|S| * H(Sv)).\n3. SVM margin = 2 / ||w||, minimized subject to yi(w.xi + b) >= 1.','Q1: State Bayes Rule and explain how a medical diagnosis agent updates disease probability given test results.\nQ2: Explain the mathematics of Information Gain and Entropy in constructing an ID3 decision tree.\nQ3: Formulate the optimization problem for a Soft-Margin Support Vector Machine.\nQ4: Differentiate between Supervised, Unsupervised, and Reinforcement Learning with real-world examples.','2026-09-03 11:37:31','2026-09-03 11:37:31'),(118,186,'Explores modern applications of AI: NLP, Machine Translation, Information Retrieval, and Autonomous Robotic Perception and Navigation.','• Natural Language Processing & Language Models (Tokenization, Embeddings, Attention)\n• Information Retrieval (Inverted Index, TF-IDF, Vector Space Model, BM25)\n• Information Extraction vs Information Retrieval\n• Speech Recognition Pipeline (Acoustic and Language Models)\n• Robotics: Sensors (LiDAR, Sonar, Vision), SLAM (Simultaneous Localization and Mapping), Configuration Space (C-space) Motion Planning','• Information Retrieval returns ranked relevant documents; Information Extraction populates structured databases from unstructured text.\n• SLAM solves the chicken-and-egg problem: building a map of an unknown environment while simultaneously tracking the robot\'s location within it.\n• Configuration space represents a robot as a single point, facilitating collision-free path planning.','1. TF-IDF = Term Frequency * Inverse Document Frequency: log(N / df).\n2. SLAM: Simultaneous Localization and Mapping using Extended Kalman Filters or Particle Filters.\n3. Motion planning algorithms: A*, Rapidly-exploring Random Trees (RRT), Visibility Graphs.','Q1: Explain the architecture of an Information Retrieval system using an inverted index and vector space model.\nQ2: What is SLAM? Why is it fundamental for autonomous mobile robots?\nQ3: Differentiate between Information Extraction (IE) and Information Retrieval (IR).\nQ4: Describe how Transformer self-attention mechanisms revolutionized machine translation.','2026-09-03 11:37:33','2026-09-03 11:37:33'),(119,187,'Covers Big Data characteristics (5 Vs), data types, distributed computing motivations, Hadoop architecture history, and ecosystem tools.','• Classification of Digital Data: Structured, Semi-Structured, Unstructured\n• The 5 Vs of Big Data: Volume, Velocity, Variety, Veracity, Value\n• Limitations of Traditional RDBMS at Terabyte/Petabyte Scale\n• Apache Hadoop Core Architecture: Storage (HDFS), Resource Management (YARN), Compute (MapReduce)\n• Hadoop Ecosystem Components (Sqoop, Flume, Hive, Pig, HBase, Spark)','• Hadoop brings computation to where data resides rather than moving massive datasets across networks.\n• Hadoop is designed to run on clusters of commodity hardware with built-in software-level fault tolerance.\n• Hadoop Streaming permits writing MapReduce jobs using any language supporting standard input/output.','1. Structured: RDBMS tables, CSV with strict schema.\n2. Semi-structured: JSON, XML, NoSQL documents with self-describing tags.\n3. Unstructured: Images, video streams, medical sensor waveforms.\n4. Core principle: Data Locality (moving computation is cheaper than moving data).','Q1: Describe the 5 Vs of Big Data with practical industrial examples.\nQ2: Differentiate between structured, semi-structured, and unstructured digital data.\nQ3: Explain the core architectural components of the Apache Hadoop Ecosystem.\nQ4: Why do traditional relational databases fail when processing high-velocity, unstructured big data?','2026-09-03 11:37:34','2026-09-03 11:37:34'),(120,188,'Deep dive into HDFS architecture, block allocation, read/write pipelines, replication strategies, and ingestion using Sqoop and Flume.','• HDFS Architecture: Master NameNode, Worker DataNodes, Secondary NameNode\n• Block Size (128 MB default) and Rack-Aware Replication Factor (3x default)\n• Anatomy of File Read and File Write in HDFS\n• Fault Tolerance: Heartbeats, Block Reports, Automatic Re-replication\n• Data Ingest Tools: Apache Sqoop (RDBMS <-> HDFS) and Apache Flume (Streaming logs <-> HDFS)\n• Hadoop I/O: SequenceFiles, Apache Avro serialization, compression codecs (Snappy, Gzip)','• NameNode stores all metadata in memory (fsimage + edits log); losing the NameNode renders the entire HDFS cluster inaccessible.\n• HDFS follows a \"Write Once, Read Many\" (WORM) access model; random in-place updates are not supported.\n• Rack awareness places replica 1 on local rack, replica 2 on same rack, and replica 3 on a separate remote rack.','1. CLI commands: hdfs dfs -ls /, hdfs dfs -put file.txt /user/data, hdfs dfs -cat /user/data/file.txt.\n2. Sqoop: import from MySQL to HDFS; export from HDFS to MySQL.\n3. Flume: Source -> Channel -> Sink architecture for continuous streaming data.','Q1: Explain the master-slave architecture of HDFS with a diagram showing NameNode, DataNodes, and Secondary NameNode.\nQ2: Trace the step-by-step data flow when an HDFS client writes a 256MB file.\nQ3: How does HDFS rack awareness optimize network bandwidth and fault tolerance?\nQ4: Differentiate between Apache Sqoop and Apache Flume regarding target data sources and architectures.','2026-09-03 11:37:35','2026-09-03 11:37:35'),(121,189,'Comprehensive guide to the MapReduce programming paradigm, YARN execution pipeline, Shuffle & Sort phase, and combiner/partitioner optimization.','• MapReduce Mathematical Transformation: Map and Reduce Signatures\n• YARN Architecture: ResourceManager, NodeManager, ApplicationMaster, Containers\n• Shuffle and Sort Phase: Spill to disk, In-memory buffer, Merging, Network fetch\n• Combiner Function: Mini-reducer applied locally to reduce network shuffle traffic\n• Partitioner: HashPartitioner determining target reducer for intermediate keys\n• InputFormats (TextInputFormat, KeyValueTextInputFormat) and RecordReaders','• Combiner functions must be commutative and associative (like Sum or Max, but not simple Average).\n• The Shuffle phase is the most network-intensive phase of a MapReduce job.\n• Speculative execution launches duplicate tasks on alternative nodes when a node runs unusually slow.','1. Map output is buffered in RAM (default 100MB); spills to disk when 80% full.\n2. HashPartitioner formula: (key.hashCode() & Integer.MAX_VALUE) % numReduceTasks.\n3. ApplicationMaster manages lifecycle of a specific application within YARN.','Q1: Trace the lifecycle of a MapReduce job execution from submission to completion under YARN.\nQ2: Explain the Shuffle and Sort phase in detail with a diagram.\nQ3: Why can a Combiner be used for counting words but cannot be directly used for calculating average temperatures?\nQ4: Write the Map and Reduce logic in pseudocode for calculating Word Count on big datasets.','2026-09-03 11:37:37','2026-09-03 11:37:37'),(122,190,'Covers higher-level query engines: Apache Pig dataflows, Apache Hive SQL data warehousing, and Apache HBase real-time columnar storage.','• Apache Pig: Execution Modes (Local, MapReduce/Tez), Grunt Shell, Pig Latin (LOAD, FILTER, GROUP, FOREACH)\n• Apache Hive: Hive Architecture (Driver, Compiler, Metastore, Execution Engine)\n• Hive Tables: Managed (Internal) vs External Tables, Partitioning, Bucketing\n• Apache HBase: Architecture (HMaster, RegionServer, HLog/WAL, MemStore, HFile)\n• Row-Oriented (RDBMS) vs Column-Family Oriented (HBase) Data Storage\n• Big SQL: Enterprise SQL on Hadoop with cost-based query optimization','• Dropping a Managed Hive table deletes both metadata and actual HDFS data; dropping an External table preserves HDFS files.\n• Hive partitioning creates physical subdirectories on HDFS, radically accelerating query filtering.\n• HBase uses row keys for fast lookups; data is stored sorted lexicographically by row key.','1. Pig Latin: A = LOAD \'data.txt\' AS (id:int, name:chararray); B = FILTER A BY id > 10; DUMP B;\n2. Hive partitioning: PARTITIONED BY (year INT, month INT) avoids full table scans.\n3. HBase write path: Client -> Write-Ahead Log (WAL) -> MemStore -> Flush to HFile on HDFS.','Q1: Compare and contrast Apache Pig, Apache Hive, and traditional RDBMS on language, execution model, and latency.\nQ2: Explain Hive Managed Tables vs External Tables with DDL commands.\nQ3: Describe the architecture and write path of Apache HBase from client to HFile.\nQ4: What is the purpose of Partitioning and Bucketing in Apache Hive performance optimization?','2026-09-03 11:37:38','2026-09-03 11:37:38'),(123,191,'Covers predictive modeling using simple and multiple linear regression, residual analysis, statistical significance, and big data visualization systems.','• Linear Regression Model Formulation: y = beta_0 + beta_1*x_1 + ... + epsilon\n• Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) Parameter Estimation\n• Interpretation of Regression Coefficients and p-values\n• Model Evaluation: R-Squared, Adjusted R-Squared, RMSE, Residual Diagnostics\n• Big Data Visualization Techniques: Density Heatmaps, Hexagonal Binning, Parallel Coordinates, Interactive Dashboards','• Correlation does not imply causation; regression identifies association patterns.\n• Multicollinearity inflates standard errors of regression coefficients, making estimates unstable.\n• Visualizing big data directly as raw scatter points causes overplotting; binning aggregation reveals underlying density.','1. Simple linear regression slope: beta_1 = Cov(x, y) / Var(x).\n2. R-squared = 1 - (SS_res / SS_tot): proportion of variance explained by the model.\n3. Residual assumptions: Normality, Homoscedasticity, Independence.','Q1: Derive the Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) estimates for the slope and intercept in simple linear regression.\nQ2: Explain the difference between R-squared and Adjusted R-squared in multiple linear regression.\nQ3: What is multicollinearity? How do you detect and resolve it in regression modeling?\nQ4: Describe three interactive visualization techniques used for analyzing high-dimensional big datasets.','2026-09-03 11:37:39','2026-09-03 11:37:39'),(124,192,'Covers the four NoSQL database categories, horizontal sharding, replication models, the CAP Theorem, and BASE eventual consistency.','• Four NoSQL Data Models: Key-Value, Document, Wide-Column, Graph\n• Schema-less Data Design and Denormalization\n• Distribution Strategies: Sharding (Hash vs Range Partitioning), Master-Slave vs Peer-to-Peer Replication\n• The CAP Theorem: Consistency, Availability, Partition Tolerance (Why CA is impossible distributed)\n• ACID vs BASE: Basically Available, Soft State, Eventual Consistency\n• Vector Clocks and Version Stamps for Conflict Resolution','• Under a network partition (P), a distributed system must choose between Consistency (CP) and Availability (AP).\n• Graph databases use index-free adjacency to traverse connections in O(1) time per edge.\n• Document stores represent hierarchical relationships via embedded documents instead of expensive joins.','1. Key-Value: high throughput caching (Redis, DynamoDB).\n2. Document: flexible JSON-like documents (MongoDB, CouchDB).\n3. Wide-column: massive sparse write throughput (Cassandra, ScyllaDB).\n4. Graph: connected nodes and edges (Neo4j, Amazon Neptune).\n5. CAP Theorem: In distributed systems, network partitions are inevitable; you must choose CP or AP.','Q1: Explain the four categories of NoSQL databases with examples and ideal use cases for each.\nQ2: State and prove the CAP Theorem. Give examples of CP, AP, and CA systems.\nQ3: Compare ACID transaction guarantees in RDBMS with BASE properties in NoSQL systems.\nQ4: Describe how Vector Clocks resolve concurrent write conflicts in peer-to-peer distributed databases.','2026-09-03 11:37:41','2026-09-03 11:37:41'),(125,193,'Introduces clinical NLP applications, unique characteristics of unstructured EHR narratives, medical preprocessing, negation detection, and patient privacy.','• Unstructured vs Structured Healthcare Data in Electronic Health Records (EHR)\n• Challenges in Clinical Text: Acronyms, Jargon, Misspellings, Negated Concepts\n• Medical Preprocessing Pipeline: Tokenization, Lemmatization, Section Segmentation\n• Negation and Uncertainty Detection (NegEx algorithm, ConText)\n• De-identification of Protected Health Information (PHI) under HIPAA Safe Harbor','• A medical symptom mentioned in a clinical note is frequently negated (e.g. \"patient denies chest pain\"); standard NLP models fail if they miss negation.\n• HIPAA specifies 18 types of Protected Health Information (PHI) that must be removed before sharing.\n• Clinical tokenizers must avoid splitting medical compounds like \"mg/kg\" or numerical dosages like \"0.5mg\".','1. NegEx algorithm: detects pre-negation and post-negation trigger phrases within a window of words.\n2. PHI: names, geographic subdivisions, dates directly related to an individual, phone numbers, medical record numbers.\n3. Clinical text representation: bag of medical words vs contextual biomedical transformer embeddings.','Q1: Why is negation detection critical when extracting symptoms from physician notes? Explain the NegEx algorithm.\nQ2: List and describe six categories of Protected Health Information (PHI) under HIPAA Safe Harbor rules.\nQ3: Explain the challenges of clinical text processing compared to general domain NLP on newspaper articles.\nQ4: Describe how clinical section segmentation helps interpret ambiguous medical findings.','2026-09-03 11:37:42','2026-09-03 11:37:42'),(126,194,'Covers medical dataset curation, clinical ontologies (UMLS, SNOMED CT, ICD-10), annotation workflows, and inter-annotator agreement evaluation.','• Benchmark Medical Corpora: MIMIC-III/IV, i2b2/n2c2 Clinical Challenges\n• Controlled Medical Vocabularies: SNOMED CT, ICD-10-CM, RxNorm, LOINC, MeSH\n• Unified Medical Language System (UMLS) and Concept Unique Identifiers (CUIs)\n• Medical Annotation Tools: brat, Prodigy, INCEpTION\n• Quality Assurance: Inter-Annotator Agreement (Cohen\'s Kappa k, Fleiss\' Kappa)','• Medical annotation requires certified clinical domain experts (doctors, nurses, medical coders).\n• Cohen\'s Kappa accounts for agreement occurring purely by chance: k = (Po - Pe) / (1 - Pe).\n• UMLS maps synonymous clinical terms across 100+ vocabularies to a single CUI.','1. SNOMED CT: comprehensive clinical healthcare terminology with poly-hierarchical relationships.\n2. ICD-10: international classification of diseases primarily used for epidemiological tracking and hospital reimbursement.\n3. Cohen\'s Kappa: k > 0.8 represents near-perfect agreement; k < 0.4 represents poor agreement.','Q1: What is the Unified Medical Language System (UMLS)? Explain how Concept Unique Identifiers (CUIs) resolve medical synonymy.\nQ2: Describe the formula and significance of Cohen\'s Kappa in evaluating medical corpus annotation quality.\nQ3: Differentiate between SNOMED CT, ICD-10, and RxNorm regarding scope and healthcare usage.\nQ4: Detail the steps required to curate an annotated clinical corpus from raw EHR progress notes.','2026-09-03 11:37:43','2026-09-03 11:37:43'),(127,195,'Covers sequence tagging for clinical NER, relation extraction for drug-event interactions, ontology entity linking, and knowledge graph construction.','• Clinical Named Entity Recognition (NER) and BIO Tagging Schemes\n• Deep Learning Sequence Tagging Architectures: BiLSTM-CRF, BioBERT, ClinicalBERT\n• Biomedical Relation Extraction (RE): Pairwise sentence-level classification of clinical relationships\n• Medical Entity Linking / Concept Normalization (String matching, TF-IDF cosine similarity, dense embedding retrieval)\n• Constructing Clinical Knowledge Graphs from extracted entities and relations','• Clinical NER must identify both entity span boundaries and fine-grained semantic categories.\n• A CRF layer on top of BiLSTM/BERT enforces valid label transition constraints (e.g. I-Disease cannot follow O without B-Disease).\n• Relation extraction determines whether a medication mentioned in a note caused an adverse reaction or was prescribed to treat it.','1. BIO tagging: B = Begin entity, I = Inside entity, O = Outside entity.\n2. Clinical relations: Treats(Drug, Disease), Causes(Drug, Adverse_Effect), Diagnosed_With(Patient, Condition).\n3. Entity normalization: maps extracted string \"aspirin 81mg\" to RxNorm code.','Q1: Illustrate BIO sequence tagging on a sample sentence: \"Patient was administered 500mg Metformin oral for Type 2 Diabetes\".\nQ2: Explain why adding a Conditional Random Field (CRF) layer enhances BiLSTM and BERT performance on clinical NER.\nQ3: Detail the pipeline for extracting Adverse Drug Events (ADEs) from clinical progress notes.\nQ4: Describe how clinical Knowledge Graphs support diagnostic reasoning in healthcare AI.','2026-09-03 11:37:45','2026-09-03 11:37:45'),(128,196,'Covers medical document classification, automated ICD-10 coding, clinical sentiment analysis, and domain-adapted Transformer models (BioBERT, ClinicalBERT).','• Clinical Text Classification Applications: Diagnostic Triage, Readmission Risk, Automated ICD-10 Billing\n• Domain-Adapted Pre-trained Transformers: BioBERT, ClinicalBERT, PubMedBERT\n• Multi-Label Classification Challenges in Long Medical Narratives\n• Attention Visualization: Explaining model predictions by highlighting salient clinical keywords\n• Evaluating Medical Classifiers: Micro/Macro F1-Score, Area Under Precision-Recall Curve (AUPRC)','• Medical datasets suffer from extreme class imbalance: rare diseases have very few training examples, while hypertension has tens of thousands.\n• Standard BERT has a 512-token limit; clinical discharge summaries often span 3,000+ words, requiring chunking or long-context Transformers (Longformer, BigBird).\n• Domain-specific pre-training on clinical text aligns token embeddings with biomedical vocabulary.','1. BioBERT: BERT architecture pre-trained on biomedical literature from PubMed and PMC.\n2. ClinicalBERT: BERT pre-trained on ICU clinical notes from MIMIC-III.\n3. Multi-label loss: Binary Cross-Entropy with Sigmoid activations on each output node.','Q1: Why does ClinicalBERT significantly outperform standard BERT-base on hospital readmission prediction tasks?\nQ2: Explain the challenge of extreme class imbalance in automated ICD-10 medical coding and how to mitigate it.\nQ3: How do you handle clinical documents that exceed the 512-token limit of Transformer encoders?\nQ4: Describe how attention weight visualization provides explainability in clinical decision support.','2026-09-03 11:37:46','2026-09-03 11:37:46'),(129,197,'Covers extractive and abstractive clinical summarization, clinical document generation, and evaluating medical factual consistency.','• Extractive Summarization (Graph-based TextRank, BERT sentence classification) vs Abstractive Summarization (Seq2Seq, LLMs)\n• Clinical Applications: Discharge Summary Synthesis, Radiology Impression Generation, Doctor-Patient Dialogue to SOAP Note\n• The Clinical Hallucination Dilemma: Fabricated medications, altered lab values, or inverted findings\n• Evaluation Metrics: ROUGE-1/2/L, BLEU, BERTScore, Fact-checking via Natural Language Inference (NLI)\n• Human-in-the-Loop Clinical Verification Workflows','• In clinical summarization, a factual hallucination can be fatal; factual correctness is vastly more important than syntactic fluency.\n• Extractive summaries are guaranteed to contain authentic physician text but can be disjointed; abstractive summaries are fluid but carry hallucination risks.\n• NLI-based factual consistency models verify that every generated sentence is strictly entailed by the original source record.','1. Extractive: selects existing sentences from source text without modification.\n2. Abstractive: generates novel sentences expressing the core concepts.\n3. ROUGE metric: measures n-gram overlap between generated summary and gold reference summary.\n4. SOAP note format: Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan.','Q1: Differentiate between extractive and abstractive summarization in the context of clinical patient records.\nQ2: What are factual hallucinations in generative clinical AI, and what mechanisms prevent them in medical deployments?\nQ3: Explain how the ROUGE and BERTScore metrics evaluate the quality of an automated radiology impression summary.\nQ4: Describe how speech-to-text combined with LLMs generates structured SOAP notes from doctor-patient consultations.','2026-09-03 11:37:47','2026-09-03 11:37:47'),(130,198,'Covers AI Clinical Decision Support Systems, conversational symptom triage agents, safety guardrails, and NLP for drug discovery and pharmacovigilance.','• Clinical Decision Support Systems (CDSS): Rule-Based vs Machine Learning-Driven Alerting\n• Longitudinal Patient Trajectory Modeling from Unstructured EHR Data\n• Healthcare Conversational Agents: Intent Classification, Entity Slot Filling, Dialogue State Tracking\n• Clinical Safety Architectures: Medical triage guardrails, handling high-risk red-flag symptoms\n• Pharmacovigilance: Mining literature, spontaneous reporting databases (FAERS), and social media for Adverse Drug Events','• CDSS alerts must avoid \"alert fatigue\" where doctors dismiss notifications due to high false-alarm rates.\n• Patient-facing medical conversational systems must NEVER provide definitive diagnostic prescriptions without human clinical review.\n• Pharmacovigilance NLP uncovers rare post-market drug side effects across diverse demographic populations.','1. CDSS: right information to the right person in the right format at the right time.\n2. Dialogue State Tracking: maintaining patient symptom history across multiple conversational turns.\n3. FAERS: FDA Adverse Event Reporting System mined using NLP relation extraction.','Q1: Explain how an NLP-powered Clinical Decision Support System flags adverse drug interactions from physician orders.\nQ2: What is \"alert fatigue\" in hospital decision support systems and how do machine learning models reduce it?\nQ3: Detail the architecture and safety guardrails required for a patient symptom triage chatbot.\nQ4: Describe the role of NLP text mining in pharmacovigilance and drug repurposing.','2026-09-03 11:37:49','2026-09-03 11:37:49'),(131,199,'Covers the history of VR, the Milgram Continuum (VR, AR, MR), hardware/software components, and human factors like simulator sickness.','• Definition and Milestones: Sensorama, Sword of Damocles, VPL Research, Oculus Rift, Meta Quest, Apple Vision Pro\n• Milgram Reality-Virtuality Continuum: Real Environment <-> AR <-> MR <-> VR\n• Degrees of Immersion: Non-Immersive, Semi-Immersive (CAVE), Fully Immersive (HMD)\n• Components of a VR System: Display, Head Tracking, Spatial Input, Game Engine, Audio\n• Human Factors & Cybersickness: Vestibular-Visual Conflict, Latency, Vergence-Accommodation Conflict','• Immersion is an objective technological property of the VR system; Presence is the subjective psychological sensation of \"being there\".\n• Simulator sickness is triggered when visual motion perceived by the eyes is not accompanied by matching vestibular inner-ear acceleration.\n• Augmented Reality overlays digital graphics on the real world; Virtual Reality completely replaces the real world with a virtual environment.','1. CAVE: Cave Automatic Virtual Environment (projection-based cube room).\n2. 3DoF: Pitch, Yaw, Roll (rotational only).\n3. 6DoF: Pitch, Yaw, Roll + X, Y, Z translation (surge, sway, heave).\n4. Motion-to-photon latency must remain below 20ms to prevent nausea.','Q1: Trace the historical evolution of Virtual Reality from early mechanical simulators to modern consumer HMDs.\nQ2: Explain the Milgram Reality-Virtuality Continuum with clear diagrams and examples of AR, MR, and VR.\nQ3: What causes cybersickness/simulator sickness in virtual reality? Describe technical methods to mitigate it.\nQ4: Differentiate between Immersion and Presence in virtual environments.','2026-09-03 11:37:50','2026-09-03 11:37:50'),(132,200,'Covers VR hardware, optical lenses, inside-out vs outside-in 6DoF tracking, stereoscopic rendering, and latency optimization.','• Tracking Architectures: 3DoF (rotational) vs 6DoF (positional), Inside-Out (SLAM) vs Outside-In (Lighthouse)\n• Display Technologies: Refresh Rate (90Hz-120Hz), Low Persistence Displays, Resolution per Eye, Screen Door Effect\n• VR Optics: Interpupillary Distance (IPD), Field of View (FOV), Fresnel vs Pancake Lenses, Chromatic Aberration Correction\n• Stereoscopic Rendering Pipeline: Two virtual cameras separated by interpupillary distance, asymmetric frustums\n• Asynchronous Timewarp (ATW) and Asynchronous Spacewarp (ASW) to maintain smooth frame rates','• Motion-to-photon latency is the time delay between physical head movement and the updated display photons reaching the eye; must be <20ms.\n• Inside-out tracking uses on-headset cameras to map environmental feature points and estimate pose via visual-inertial odometry.\n• Asynchronous Timewarp re-projects the rendered frame based on the latest head orientation just before display refresh, preventing dropped frames.','1. 6DoF: Pitch, Yaw, Roll, X, Y, Z.\n2. IMU: Accelerometer (measures gravity/acceleration) + Gyroscope (angular velocity) + Magnetometer (compass heading).\n3. Target frame rate: strictly 90 FPS or higher to prevent visual stutter.','Q1: Explain the difference between Inside-Out and Outside-In tracking architectures with diagrams.\nQ2: What is the Screen Door Effect in VR displays, and how do modern high-PPD panels eliminate it?\nQ3: Explain the concept and significance of Interpupillary Distance (IPD) adjustment in VR headsets.\nQ4: Describe how Asynchronous Timewarp (ATW) safeguards user comfort when an application drops frames.','2026-09-03 11:37:51','2026-09-03 11:37:51'),(133,201,'Covers the three core 3D interaction tasks: Navigation (locomotion/teleportation), Selection, and Manipulation, alongside haptics and 3D UI design.','• The Three Core Tasks of 3D Interaction: Navigation (Travel & Wayfinding), Selection, Manipulation\n• Locomotion Paradigms: Discrete Teleportation (Parabolic Arc), Continuous Smooth Locomotion, Room-Scale Walking\n• Manipulation Techniques: Direct Touch/Virtual Hand, Ray-Casting with Snap-to-Target, Virtual Hand Extensions (Go-Go technique)\n• Sensory Feedback in VR: Visual Feedback (Highlights, Reticles), Audio (Spatial feedback), Haptic (Vibrotactile waveforms)\n• 3D User Interfaces: Diegetic UI (integrated in-world), Non-Diegetic, Spatial Menus, Hand-Attached Wrist Palettes','• Teleportation with instant fading avoids optical flow acceleration, keeping the vestibular system undisturbed.\n• Ray-casting is fast for distant selections; direct grabbing is intuitive for near-field precision work.\n• Diegetic UI elements (like a watch display or physical cockpit switches) enhance presence and immersion.','1. Travel: physical motor movement through virtual space; Wayfinding: cognitive navigation and mental mapping.\n2. Teleportation arc: physics trajectory projecting where the user will land.\n3. Degrees of manipulation: 6DoF translation (X,Y,Z) + rotation (Pitch,Yaw,Roll).','Q1: Explain the difference between continuous locomotion and teleportation in VR, highlighting pros and cons of each.\nQ2: Compare Direct Grabbing vs Ray-Casting for 3D object manipulation with interaction diagrams.\nQ3: What are Diegetic, Non-Diegetic, and Spatial user interfaces in VR? Provide an example of each.\nQ4: Describe how multi-modal haptic and auditory feedback reinforces virtual object manipulation.','2026-09-03 11:37:53','2026-09-03 11:37:53'),(134,202,'Explores virtual world construction, PBR rendering, Mel Slater presence illusions, binaural audio acoustics, and VR usability heuristics.','• The Duality of Presence: Place Illusion (PI) and Plausibility Illusion (Psi)\n• Architectural Ergonomics: Human scale (1 unit = 1 meter), eye-level calibration, comfortable reach zones\n• Physically Based Rendering (PBR): Albedo, Normal maps, Roughness, Metallic channels\n• Spatial Sound Engineering: HRTF (Head-Related Transfer Function), Interaural Time Difference (ITD), Interaural Level Difference (ILD)\n• Acoustics: Sound Occlusion, Obstruction, Reverberation, Early Reflections\n• VR Usability Heuristics: Preventing fatigue, clear affordances, avoiding neck strain (>30 degree gaze angles)','• In VR, scale is critical: if a table is 5% too tall, the user immediately feels disoriented like a child.\n• Spatial audio is 50% of the immersion equation; believable 3D sound anchors visual objects in physical space.\n• Baked static lighting delivers photorealistic ambient occlusion without burning mobile GPU cycles at runtime.','1. ITD: sound arrives at the closer ear microseconds before the further ear.\n2. ILD: the head creates an acoustic shadow, making sound louder in the closer ear.\n3. Ergonomic comfort: interactables should be placed between chest and waist height within 0.5m of user.','Q1: Explain Mel Slater\'s Place Illusion (PI) and Plausibility Illusion (Psi) and how each is achieved in VR design.\nQ2: Describe how Head-Related Transfer Functions (HRTFs) synthesize realistic 3D spatial audio for stereo headphones.\nQ3: What are the best practices for lighting and material optimization in standalone VR environments?\nQ4: List five critical VR User Experience (UX) design heuristics that prevent user fatigue and disorientation.','2026-09-03 11:37:54','2026-09-03 11:37:54'),(135,203,'Surveys real-world applications of VR in surgical simulation, psychiatry, aviation training, architecture, and social collaboration.','• Medical VR: Pre-Operative Planning, Surgical Simulation with Force Feedback, Burn Patient Pain Distraction (SnowWorld)\n• Psychiatric Applications: Virtual Reality Exposure Therapy (VRET) for Phobias (Arachnophobia, Acrophobia, PTSD)\n• Industrial & Military Training: Flight Simulators, Hazardous Waste Handling, Nuclear Plant Safety Drills\n• Architectural & Engineering CAD Visualization and Digital Twin Walkthroughs\n• Social VR & Telepresence: Spatial Audio, Non-Verbal Eye/Face Tracking, Photorealistic Digital Avatars','• VR surgical simulation allows trainees to commit critical mistakes without risk to human life.\n• Immersion in interactive snowy VR worlds acts as non-pharmacological analgesic pain relief by flooding cognitive attention bandwidth.\n• Virtual industrial training reduces corporate training costs by 70% while improving memory retention.','1. VRET: graded exposure to fearful stimuli in an immersive, clinician-controlled virtual environment.\n2. Telepresence: feeling present with remote colleagues in a shared digital workspace.\n3. Digital Twins: real-time 3D VR simulation mirroring a physical factory or jet engine.','Q1: Explain how Virtual Reality Exposure Therapy (VRET) treats anxiety disorders and phobias.\nQ2: Describe how haptic feedback surgical simulators evaluate surgeon skill metrics.\nQ3: What are the economic and safety advantages of VR simulation in aviation and heavy industrial training?\nQ4: Discuss the architectural requirements of a Social VR platform supporting multi-user collaboration.','2026-09-03 11:37:55','2026-09-03 11:37:55'),(136,204,'Hands-on VR development guide covering OpenXR, Unity XR Interaction Toolkit, locomotion, grabbing mechanics, and mobile VR performance optimization.','• VR Development Frameworks: Unity3D, Unreal Engine, OpenXR Industry Standard API\n• The XR Rig (XR Origin): Tracking space, Main Camera (HMD), Left/Right Controller Hand Anchors\n• Unity XR Interaction Toolkit (XRI): XR Interaction Manager, XR Direct Interactor, XR Ray Interactor\n• Grabbable Objects: XR Grab Interactable, Colliders, Rigidbodies, Throw Physics (Velocity Tracking)\n• Locomotion System: Teleportation Provider, Snap Turn / Continuous Turn, Continuous Move Provider\n• Performance Optimization: Draw Call Batching (Static/Dynamic/GPU Instancing), Occlusion Culling, Single-Pass Stereo Instanced Rendering','• OpenXR eliminates fragmentation: develop one codebase that deploys to Meta Quest, HTC Vive, Pico, Valve Index, and Windows Mixed Reality.\n• Single-Pass Stereo Instancing renders both left and right eye views in a single GPU draw call, doubling rendering efficiency.\n• Physics updates in VR must run on FixedUpdate at identical frequency to display refresh (e.g. 90Hz) to prevent controller tracking jitter.','1. OpenXR: royalty-free open standard from Khronos Group for AR/VR runtime interoperability.\n2. XR Grab Interactable: attach to any GameObject with Collider + Rigidbody to enable pickup.\n3. Teleportation Area: surface where user can teleport anywhere; Teleportation Anchor: specific landing point with locked orientation.','Q1: Detail the step-by-step setup of a modern VR application in Unity3D using OpenXR and XR Interaction Toolkit.\nQ2: Explain the difference between an XR Direct Interactor, an XR Ray Interactor, and an XR Socket Interactor.\nQ3: What is Single-Pass Instanced Stereo Rendering, and how does it optimize GPU rendering performance in VR?\nQ4: Describe four techniques to reduce draw calls when building a complex VR scene for a standalone mobile headset.','2026-09-03 11:37:57','2026-09-03 11:37:57'),(137,206,'This dedicated topic lecture explores \'Definition of compiler and interpreter and differences\' within the module \'Introduction to Compilers\' of Compiler Design. It analyzes theoretical foundations, architectural mechanisms, practical algorithms, and industry implementation practices.','• Foundational principles and formal definition of Definition of compiler and interpreter and differences.\n• Mathematical modeling, workflow pipelines, and structural properties in Compiler Design.\n• Algorithmic procedures, state representations, and execution constraints.\n• Comparative trade-offs, edge-case behavior, and optimization strategies.','• Definition of compiler and interpreter and differences is a high-yield syllabus topic regularly evaluated in university exams and technical interviews.\n• Understanding the underlying logic of Definition of compiler and interpreter and differences enables deeper comprehension of subsequent advanced modules in Compiler Design.\n• Pay close attention to standard constraints, performance benchmarks, and common failure modes.','1. Core Definition: Memorize the precise definition and purpose of Definition of compiler and interpreter and differences.\n2. Methodological Steps: Review the step-by-step procedure or algorithm demonstrated in the lecture.\n3. Practical Formula/Rules: Check all governing equations, transformation rules, and time/space complexity.\n4. Key Contrast: Differentiate Definition of compiler and interpreter and differences from its related counterparts in Introduction to Compilers.','Q1: Explain the fundamental concept of Definition of compiler and interpreter and differences with a suitable diagram and real-world example.\nQ2: What are the primary advantages, challenges, and constraints associated with Definition of compiler and interpreter and differences in Compiler Design?\nQ3: Step through the execution or algorithmic procedure of Definition of compiler and interpreter and differences on a sample problem instance.\nQ4: Compare and contrast Definition of compiler and interpreter and differences with alternative paradigms covered in Introduction to Compilers.','2026-09-10 08:37:50','2026-09-10 08:37:50'),(138,207,'This dedicated topic lecture explores \'Phases of a compiler and translation passes\' within the module \'Introduction to Compilers\' of Compiler Design. It analyzes theoretical foundations, architectural mechanisms, practical algorithms, and industry implementation practices.','• Foundational principles and formal definition of Phases of a compiler and translation passes.\n• Mathematical modeling, workflow pipelines, and structural properties in Compiler Design.\n• Algorithmic procedures, state representations, and execution constraints.\