tate transition maintains strict invariant properties to ensure correctness. When evaluating computational complexity, asymptotic time complexity and spatial overhead must both be factored in. By tracing through concrete dry-run examples, you will observe the exact trade-offs between memory density, CPU cycles, and algorithmic clarity. Mastering this foundational topic prepares you to tackle both theoretical engineering exams and high-stakes coding assessments.','2026-09-01 07:26:29','2026-09-01 07:26:29'),(73,106,'This lecture covers the fundamentals of Database Systems Significance and Advantages. \n\nKey topics discussed in this lecture:\n\n1. Introduction to Database Systems Significance and Advantages - Understanding the core concepts and why they matter in computer science and software engineering.\n\n2. Core Concepts - The fundamental building blocks and principles that form the foundation of Database Systems Significance and Advantages.\n\n3. Implementation Details - Step-by-step walkthrough of how Database Systems Significance and Advantages works in practice, including common operations and their time complexity.\n\n4. Examples and Applications - Real-world use cases and practical examples demonstrating the application of Database Systems Significance and Advantages.\n\n5. Common Mistakes - Pitfalls to avoid when working with Database Systems Significance and Advantages, including performance issues and logical errors.\n\n6. Summary - Quick recap of the key takeaways and what you should remember about Database Systems Significance and Advantages.\n\nThis lecture is designed to provide a comprehensive understanding of Database Systems Significance and Advantages and prepare you for the upcoming assignment.','2026-09-03 11:29:56','2026-09-03 11:29:56'),(74,108,'This lecture covers the fundamentals of Basic Concepts of Indexing and Ordered Indices. \n\nKey topics discussed in this lecture:\n\n1. Introduction to Basic Concepts of Indexing and Ordered Indices - Understanding the core concepts and why they matter in computer science and software engineering.\n\n2. Core Concepts - The fundamental building blocks and principles that form the foundation of Basic Concepts of Indexing and Ordered Indices.\n\n3. Implementation Details - Step-by-step walkthrough of how Basic Concepts of Indexing and Ordered Indices works in practice, including common operations and their time complexity.\n\n4. Examples and Applications - Real-world use cases and practical examples demonstrating the application of Basic Concepts of Indexing and Ordered Indices.\n\n5. Common Mistakes - Pitfalls to avoid when working with Basic Concepts of Indexing and Ordered Indices, including performance issues and logical errors.\n\n6. Summary - Quick recap of the key takeaways and what you should remember about Basic Concepts of Indexing and Ordered Indices.\n\nThis lecture is designed to provide a comprehensive understanding of Basic Concepts of Indexing and Ordered Indices and prepare you for the upcoming assignment.','2026-09-03 11:30:00','2026-09-03 11:30:00'),(75,110,'This lecture covers the fundamentals of Denormalization and Database Design. \n\nKey topics discussed in this lecture:\n\n1. Introduction to Denormalization and Database Design - Understanding the core concepts and why they matter in computer science and software engineering.\n\n2. Core Concepts - The fundamental building blocks and principles that form the foundation of Denormalization and Database Design.\n\n3. Implementation Details - Step-by-step walkthrough of how Denormalization and Database Design works in practice, including common operations and their time complexity.\n\n4. Examples and Applications - Real-world use cases and practical examples demonstrating the application of Denormalization and Database Design.\n\n5. Common Mistakes - Pitfalls to avoid when working with Denormalization and Database Design, including performance issues and logical errors.\n\n6. Summary - Quick recap of the key takeaways and what you should remember about Denormalization and Database Design.\n\nThis lecture is designed to provide a comprehensive understanding of Denormalization and Database Design and prepare you for the upcoming assignment.','2026-09-03 11:30:05','2026-09-03 11:30:05'),(76,112,'This lecture covers the fundamentals of Deadlock Handling and Time-stamp Based Protocols. \n\nKey topics discussed in this lecture:\n\n1. Introduction to Deadlock Handling and Time-stamp Based Protocols - Understanding the core concepts and why they matter in computer science and software engineering.\n\n2. Core Concepts - The fundamental building blocks and principles that form the foundation of Deadlock Handling and Time-stamp Based Protocols.\n\n3. Implementation Details - Step-by-step walkthrough of how Deadlock Handling and Time-stamp Based Protocols works in practice, including common operations and their time complexity.\n\n4. Examples and Applications - Real-world use cases and practical examples demonstrating the application of Deadlock Handling and Time-stamp Based Protocols.\n\n5. Common Mistakes - Pitfalls to avoid when working with Deadlock Handling and Time-stamp Based Protocols, including performance issues and logical errors.\n\n6. Summary - Quick recap of the key takeaways and what you should remember about Deadlock Handling and Time-stamp Based Protocols.\n\nThis lecture is designed to provide a comprehensive understanding of Deadlock Handling and Time-stamp Based Protocols and prepare you for the upcoming assignment.','2026-09-03 11:30:09','2026-09-03 11:30:09'),(77,114,'This lecture covers the fundamentals of Backtracking: 8-Queen Problem. \n\nKey topics discussed in this lecture:\n\n1. Introduction to Backtracking: 8-Queen Problem - Understanding the core concepts and why they matter in computer science and software engineering.\n\n2. Core Concepts - The fundamental building blocks and principles that form the foundation of Backtracking: 8-Queen Problem.\n\n3. Implementation Details - Step-by-step walkthrough of how Backtracking: 8-Queen Problem works in practice, including common operations and their time complexity.\n\n4. Examples and Applications - Real-world use cases and practical examples demonstrating the application of Backtracking: 8-Queen Problem.\n\n5. Common Mistakes - Pitfalls to avoid when working with Backtracking: 8-Queen Problem, including performance issues and logical errors.\n\n6. Summary - Quick recap of the key takeaways and what you should remember about Backtracking: 8-Queen Problem.\n\nThis lecture is designed to provide a comprehensive understanding of Backtracking: 8-Queen Problem and prepare you for the upcoming assignment.','2026-09-03 11:30:13','2026-09-03 11:30:13'),(78,116,'This lecture covers the fundamentals of Authorization and Advanced SQL. \n\nKey topics discussed in this lecture:\n\n1. Introduction to Authorization and Advanced SQL - Understanding the core concepts and why they matter in computer science and software engineering.\n\n2. Core Concepts - The fundamental building blocks and principles that form the foundation of Authorization and Advanced SQL.\n\n3. Implementation Details - Step-by-step walkthrough of how Authorization and Advanced SQL works in practice, including common operations and their time complexity.\n\n4. Examples and Applications - Real-world use cases and practical examples demonstrating the application of Authorization and Advanced SQL.\n\n5. Common Mistakes - Pitfalls to avoid when working with Authorization and Advanced SQL, including performance issues and logical errors.\n\n6. Summary - Quick recap of the key takeaways and what you should remember about Authorization and Advanced SQL.\n\nThis lecture is designed to provide a comprehensive understanding of Authorization and Advanced SQL and prepare you for the upcoming assignment.','2026-09-03 11:30:17','2026-09-03 11:30:17'),(108,176,'Welcome to this in-depth lecture on Compiler Design. In this module, we examine how high-level programming language code transforms into executable machine binaries. A compiler translates the entire source program into target code prior to execution, whereas an interpreter translates and executes line-by-line. The compilation pipeline is partitioned into analysis (front end) and synthesis (back end). In lexical analysis, source text is scanned into tokens using regular expressions and deterministic finite automata (DFA). We address grammar ambiguities such as left recursion and left factoring to construct deterministic top-down predictive LL(1) parsers.','2026-09-03 11:37:19','2026-09-03 11:37:19'),(109,177,'In this session, we investigate Bottom-Up Parsing, which constructs parse trees starting from leaves up to the root non-terminal. We implement shift-reduce parsing using an explicit parser stack. A \"handle\" is a substring matching the right-hand side of a production whose reduction represents one step along the reverse of a rightmost derivation. We evaluate LR parsers: SLR(1) uses FOLLOW sets for reduce decisions; CLR(1) embeds explicit lookahead tokens into LR items; LALR(1) merges states with identical item cores to reduce memory footprint. Finally, we explore YACC for automatic parser generation.','2026-09-03 11:37:20','2026-09-03 11:37:20'),(110,178,'This lecture covers Syntax Directed Translation (SDT) and Intermediate Code Generation (ICG). Syntax Directed Definitions associate grammar productions with semantic rules. We classify attributes as synthesized (computed from children) or inherited (computed from parent or siblings). In S-attributed SDDs, all attributes are synthesized and can be evaluated during bottom-up parsing. In L-attributed definitions, dependency arrows flow left-to-right. We then generate machine-independent intermediate code: Abstract Syntax Trees, Postfix notation, and Three-Address Code (TAC) represented via quadruples, triples, and indirect triples.','2026-09-03 11:37:22','2026-09-03 11:37:22'),(111,179,'In this lecture, we examine Type Checking and Runtime Storage Environments. A type system enforces language semantics and guarantees memory safety. Type checkers verify that operations receive valid operand types, performing implicit coercions where valid. In Runtime Environments, the operating system allocates memory into code, static data, call stack, and heap. Each function invocation creates an activation record (stack frame) containing return address, parameters, local variables, and saved registers. We explore access links and displays for accessing non-local variables in lexically scoped languages.','2026-09-03 11:37:23','2026-09-03 11:37:23'),(112,180,'In our final module on Compiler Design, we explore Code Optimization. The optimizer transforms intermediate code into equivalent representations that consume fewer CPU cycles and less memory. A Basic Block is a sequence of three-address statements entered only at the first statement and exited only at the last. By linking basic blocks with directed edges, we construct the Control Flow Graph (CFG). We construct Directed Acyclic Graphs (DAGs) to identify local common subexpressions, dead code, and temporary variable reuse. Finally, we formulate Global Data Flow Equations (Reaching Definitions, Live Variable Analysis) using iterative data flow frameworks.','2026-09-03 11:37:24','2026-09-03 11:37:24'),(113,181,'Welcome to Artificial Intelligence. In this opening module, we define artificial intelligence and investigate what constitutes an intelligent agent. An agent perceives its environment through sensors and acts upon it via actuators. We formalize task environments using the PEAS model: Performance measure, Environment, Actuators, and Sensors. We contrast simple reflex agents, model-based agents with internal memory, goal-driven agents, and utility-maximizing agents. Finally, we formulate AI search problems with initial states, actions, transition models, goal tests, and path cost functions.','2026-09-03 11:37:26','2026-09-03 11:37:26'),(114,182,'This lecture examines search algorithms that solve complex AI problems. We contrast uninformed search with heuristic search. A* search evaluates f(n) = g(n) + h(n), balancing path cost g(n) with estimated distance h(n). When h(n) is admissible (never overestimating true cost) and consistent (satisfying triangle inequality), A* is complete and optimal. We address local optimization using Hill Climbing and Simulated Annealing. In Constraint Satisfaction Problems (CSP), we explore backtracking with Arc Consistency (AC-3). Finally, we analyze Adversarial Game Playing using Minimax and Alpha-Beta Pruning.','2026-09-03 11:37:27','2026-09-03 11:37:27'),(115,183,'In this session, we investigate Knowledge Representation and Automated Reasoning. Propositional logic lacks expressiveness for quantified objects and relations; hence we adopt First Order Predicate Logic (FOPL) with quantifiers, constants, predicates, and functions. We study inference engines using Generalized Modus Ponens, Forward Chaining (data-driven), and Backward Chaining (goal-driven, used in Prolog). We examine the Unification algorithm and Robinson\'s Resolution Refutation method in Conjunctive Normal Form (CNF). Finally, we review Ontological Engineering, category hierarchies, semantic networks, and default reasoning.','2026-09-03 11:37:28','2026-09-03 11:37:28'),(116,184,'This lecture focuses on Multi-Agent Systems (MAS) and Distributed AI. When problems are distributed geographically, computationally, or administratively, multiple autonomous software agents must coordinate. We study agent communication architectures including speech act theory, KQML, and FIPA-ACL. We examine negotiation protocols such as Contract Net for task allocation, Rubinstein alternating offers bargaining, and argumentation frameworks. Finally, we formulate mathematical trust and reputation models (Beta reputation, EigenTrust) to secure multi-agent systems against malicious or deceptive agents.','2026-09-03 11:37:30','2026-09-03 11:37:30'),(117,185,'In this lecture, we study learning processes and probabilistic reasoning. In real environments, agents possess incomplete and uncertain knowledge. We apply probability axioms, joint probability distributions, and Bayes Theorem to perform probabilistic inference. We classify machine learning into supervised learning (labeled targets), unsupervised learning (clustering patterns), and reinforcement learning (reward signals). We examine foundational supervised models: Linear Regression via gradient descent, Support Vector Machines maximizing functional margins with kernel tricks, and tree-based models using Information Gain and Gini Impurity.','2026-09-03 11:37:31','2026-09-03 11:37:31'),(118,186,'Our concluding module explores applied AI systems across perception, language, and physical robotics. In language systems, we study the evolution from statistical n-gram language models to modern Transformer architectures. We examine Information Retrieval engines using inverted indices and TF-IDF/BM25 scoring, alongside Information Extraction for entity and relation extraction. In physical agents, we investigate robotic hardware, perception using LiDAR and stereo vision, probabilistic localization using particle filters, Simultaneous Localization and Mapping (SLAM), and configuration space motion planning.','2026-09-03 11:37:33','2026-09-03 11:37:33'),(119,187,'Welcome to Big Data Analysis. In this foundational module, we analyze the characteristics of Big Data. Data is classified as structured (relational tables), semi-structured (XML, JSON, server logs), or unstructured (audio, video, raw text). We examine the 5 Vs: Volume, Velocity, Variety, Veracity, and Value. We trace the history of Apache Hadoop from Google GFS and MapReduce whitepapers. We contrast data analysis using classic Unix shell pipes against Hadoop distributed clusters. Finally, we survey the Hadoop ecosystem: HDFS, YARN, MapReduce, Pig, Hive, HBase, Spark, and enterprise platforms like IBM BigInsights.','2026-09-03 11:37:34','2026-09-03 11:37:34'),(120,188,'This lecture investigates HDFS (Hadoop Distributed File System). HDFS is designed for storing very large files with streaming data access patterns on commodity clusters. Files are split into large blocks (default 128MB) replicated across distinct racks (default replication factor 3). We examine the master-worker topology: NameNode manages namespace and block locations in RAM; DataNodes store and serve actual blocks. We trace the complete read and write pipeline, rack awareness, and checkpointing by the Secondary NameNode. We explore ingestion tools: Apache Sqoop for RDBMS transfer and Apache Flume for streaming web logs.','2026-09-03 11:37:35','2026-09-03 11:37:35'),(121,189,'In this lecture, we examine MapReduce, Hadoop\'s original distributed parallel compute engine. The MapReduce model processes data as key-value pairs: Map: (k1, v1) -> list(k2, v2), and Reduce: (k2, list(v2)) -> list(k3, v3). We analyze the anatomy of a job execution under YARN: ResourceManager allocates container resources, while NodeManagers launch tasks. We examine the Shuffle and Sort phase where intermediate map outputs are partitioned by key, sorted, and transferred over the network to reducers. We explore performance optimizations: in-mapper combining, custom partitioners, and the distributed cache.','2026-09-03 11:37:37','2026-09-03 11:37:37'),(122,190,'In this session, we investigate higher-level Hadoop ecosystem tools: Pig, Hive, HBase, and Big SQL. Writing raw Java MapReduce code is verbose and complex; Apache Pig introduces Pig Latin, an imperative dataflow language compiled into MapReduce DAGs. Apache Hive provides HiveQL, a declarative SQL dialect that allows data analysts to query petabytes stored on HDFS without writing code. We contrast Hive Managed Tables with External Tables. We then examine Apache HBase, a distributed, versioned, column-oriented NoSQL database modeled after Google Bigtable, delivering sub-millisecond random read/write lookups on billions of rows.','2026-09-03 11:37:38','2026-09-03 11:37:38'),(123,191,'This lecture covers Predictive Analytics and Visual Data Analysis for Big Data. Predictive analytics uncovers patterns in historical data to forecast future events. We examine Linear Regression, modeling relationships between dependent target variables and independent predictors. We formulate Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) to minimize the sum of squared residuals. In Multiple Linear Regression, we interpret coefficients, test for multicollinearity (VIF), and evaluate goodness of fit with R-squared and Adjusted R-squared. Finally, we explore visual interaction techniques (brushing, zooming, linked views) for analyzing high-dimensional big data.','2026-09-03 11:37:39','2026-09-03 11:37:39'),(124,192,'In our final module on Big Data, we explore NoSQL Databases and Distributed Data Modeling. Traditional relational databases enforce ACID transactions with rigid schemas, creating bottlenecks for horizontal scaling. NoSQL architectures embrace Aggregate Data Models: Key-Value stores (Redis), Document databases (MongoDB), Column-Family stores (Cassandra), and Graph databases (Neo4j). We explore distributed data distribution: Sharding for horizontal partitioning, and Replication (Master-Slave vs Peer-to-Peer). We study Brewer\'s CAP Theorem and BASE properties (Basically Available, Soft state, Eventual consistency), alongside vector clocks for version reconciliation.','2026-09-03 11:37:41','2026-09-03 11:37:41'),(125,193,'Welcome to NLP for Health-Related Text. In healthcare, over 80% of critical patient information resides in unstructured clinical narratives—physician progress notes, radiology reports, and pathology summaries. In this opening module, we analyze the challenges of biomedical NLP: non-standard abbreviations, ambiguous clinical shorthand, telegraphic sentence fragments, and typographical errors. We establish clinical text preprocessing pipelines including specialized sentence splitting, medical tokenization, and negation detection (e.g. NegEx). Finally, we explore HIPAA regulations and de-identification algorithms that remove Protected Health Information (PHI).','2026-09-03 11:37:42','2026-09-03 11:37:42'),(126,194,'In this lecture, we study the methodology of building high-quality clinical corpora. Supervised medical machine learning requires rigorously annotated datasets. We explore open-access clinical data repositories like MIMIC-III and MIMIC-IV from MIT, alongside biomedical literature from PubMed Central. We examine standard medical vocabularies: SNOMED CT for clinical findings, ICD-10 for diagnostic billing, RxNorm for medications, and MeSH for indexing. We examine the UMLS Metathesaurus that unifies these ontologies. We detail manual annotation workflows using tools like brat, formulation of annotation guidelines, and inter-rater agreement measurement via Cohen\'s Kappa and Fleiss\' Kappa.','2026-09-03 11:37:43','2026-09-03 11:37:43'),(127,195,'In this lecture, we study Clinical Information Extraction (IE). Information extraction converts unstructured clinical text into structured relational records. We formulate Named Entity Recognition (NER) as sequence labeling (IOB/BIO schemes) to detect medical entities: diseases, anatomical sites, pharmaceuticals, dosages, routes, and temporal markers. We explore neural architectures: BiLSTM-CRF and fine-tuned BioBERT. We then investigate Biomedical Relation Extraction (RE) to identify interactions such as Drug-Cause-Adverse Event or Treatment-Treats-Condition. Finally, we link entities to ontology concepts and construct clinical Knowledge Graphs.','2026-09-03 11:37:45','2026-09-03 11:37:45'),(128,196,'This session explores Clinical Text Classification. Medical institutions generate millions of documents requiring categorization: triaging emergency admissions, assigning ICD-10 diagnostic billing codes, and analyzing patient satisfaction surveys. We contrast traditional bag-of-words and TF-IDF classifiers with deep Transformer representations. General BERT models trained on Wikipedia fail on clinical jargon; hence we examine domain-specific pre-trained models: BioBERT (trained on PubMed abstracts) and ClinicalBERT (trained on MIMIC clinical notes). We analyze multi-label classification architectures designed to predict hundreds of co-occurring ICD codes from lengthy hospital discharge summaries.','2026-09-03 11:37:46','2026-09-03 11:37:46'),(129,197,'In this lecture, we examine Clinical Text Summarization and Medical Text Generation. Clinicians spend over two hours documenting notes for every hour spent with patients. We examine Extractive Summarization, which scores and selects salient sentences from medical histories, and Abstractive Summarization using sequence-to-sequence Transformers (BART, T5, LLMs) that generate coherent narrative summaries. We examine key clinical generation use cases: synthesizing multi-day hospital stays into discharge summaries, and generating radiology report impressions from findings sections. Finally, we address the critical challenge of factual hallucinations and explore specialized medical evaluation metrics.','2026-09-03 11:37:47','2026-09-03 11:37:47'),(130,198,'In our concluding module, we study integrated NLP applications in healthcare: Clinical Decision Support Systems (CDSS), conversational virtual assistants, and pharmacovigilance. CDSS systems analyze real-time patient charts to alert physicians about drug-drug interactions, sepsis onset, or missed clinical guidelines. We examine patient-facing healthcare chatbots: symptom triage protocols, intent classification, entity extraction, and clinical safety guardrails that escalate urgent symptoms to emergency services. Finally, we explore NLP in pharmacovigilance: mining PubMed, clinical trial registries, and patient forums to detect Adverse Drug Reactions (ADRs) long before formal recalls.','2026-09-03 11:37:49','2026-09-03 11:37:49'),(131,199,'Welcome to Introduction to Virtual Reality and 3D Interaction. Virtual Reality is an artificial, computer-generated simulation of a 3D environment with which a person can interact using specialized electronic devices. We trace the history of VR: Morton Heilig\'s 1956 Sensorama, Ivan Sutherland\'s 1968 Sword of Damocles, up to modern standalone headsets. We distinguish between non-immersive VR (desktop), semi-immersive (flight simulators, CAVE systems), and fully immersive VR (HMDs with 6DoF tracking). We analyze the Milgram Reality-Virtuality Continuum connecting Real World, Augmented Reality (AR), Augmented Virtuality (AV), and Pure Virtual Reality. Finally, we explore human perceptual factors, latency, and simulator sickness.','2026-09-03 11:37:50','2026-09-03 11:37:50'),(132,200,'In this lecture, we examine VR Hardware Architecture and 3D Rendering Systems. A high-performance VR headset integrates ultra-fast displays, custom lenses, and high-frequency tracking sensors. We explore display technologies: dual OLED and fast-switch LCD panels with high pixel density to eliminate the \"screen-door effect\". We analyze lens physics: Fresnel lenses vs pancake optical assemblies. We contrast tracking mechanisms: outside-in lighthouse tracking using infrared lasers versus inside-out computer vision tracking using optical cameras and SLAM on the headset. Finally, we trace the stereoscopic rendering pipeline, time-warp / reprojection, and strict latency budgets.','2026-09-03 11:37:51','2026-09-03 11:37:51'),(133,201,'In this lecture, we explore 3D Interaction Techniques in Virtual Reality. Interaction in 3D space departs radically from 2D desktop mouse-and-keyboard metaphors. We analyze the three fundamental tasks of 3D interaction: Navigation, Selection, and Manipulation. In navigation, we contrast continuous locomotion with discrete Teleportation paradigms (parabolic ray arc) and dynamic vignetting to prevent cybersickness. In selection and manipulation, we analyze Direct Virtual Hand Grabbing versus Ray-Casting for distant objects. Finally, we investigate multi-modal sensory feedback—visual highlights, spatial audio clicks, and vibrotactile haptic pulses—and examine spatial 3D UI architectures.','2026-09-03 11:37:53','2026-09-03 11:37:53'),(134,202,'In this lecture, we examine VR Environment Design and Spatial Audio. Creating compelling virtual environments requires a mastery of architectural spatial design, real-time lighting, and psychoacoustics. We analyze Mel Slater\'s theory of Presence: the Place Illusion (PI - the illusion of being there) and the Plausibility Illusion (Psi - the illusion that events occurring in the virtual world are really happening). We study real-time lighting: baked global illumination lightmaps, Physically Based Rendering (PBR) materials, and realistic scale. We dive deep into Spatial Audio: Head-Related Transfer Functions (HRTF), binaural audio, sound reflection, and Doppler shift.','2026-09-03 11:37:54','2026-09-03 11:37:54'),(135,203,'This lecture surveys the transformative real-world applications of Virtual Reality. In medicine, VR powers high-fidelity surgical simulators with haptic feedback, allowing surgeons to practice rare procedures without patient risk. In clinical psychiatry, Virtual Reality Exposure Therapy (VRET) treats PTSD, phobias, and anxiety through controlled stimulus exposure. In industrial training, aviation and heavy equipment operators train in hazardous scenarios—emergency fire evacuations, chemical plant failures—safely and cost-effectively. In enterprise and education, Social VR platforms enable global collaborative design reviews and virtual classrooms. Finally, we analyze convergence with Artificial Intelligence and spatial computing.','2026-09-03 11:37:55','2026-09-03 11:37:55'),(136,204,'In our final module, we learn how to build and optimize Virtual Reality applications using industry-standard game engines. We focus on Unity3D and the OpenXR cross-platform standard. We configure the XR Interaction Toolkit: establishing the XR Origin rig, mapping head and hand controllers, and setting up interaction managers. We implement Core VR Interactors: XR Direct Interactor for grabbing near objects, XR Ray Interactor for teleportation locomotion and UI clicks, and Socket Interactors for snap-in inventory mechanics. Finally, we deep dive into performance profiling: reducing draw calls via static and dynamic batching, occlusion culling, and shader optimization for mobile headsets.','2026-09-03 11:37:57','2026-09-03 11:37:57'),(137,206,'Welcome to this focused lecture on Definition of compiler and interpreter and differences, part of the Introduction to Compilers curriculum in Compiler Design. In today\'s session, we break down Definition of compiler and interpreter and differences step-by-step. We begin by examining the underlying motivation and academic significance of this topic. Next, we trace the formal methodology, illustrating how the core mechanisms operate in practical systems. We analyze standard examples, clarify potential pitfalls, and discuss optimization considerations. By the end of this lecture, you will have a thorough mastery of Definition of compiler and interpreter and differences ready for assignments and exams.','2026-09-10 08:37:50','2026-09-10 08:37:50'),(138,207,'Welcome to this focused lecture on Phases of a compiler and translation passes, part of the Introduction to Compilers curriculum in Compiler Design. In today\'s session, we break down Phases of a compiler and translation passes step-by-step. We begin by examining the underlying motivation and academic significance of this topic. Next, we trace the formal methodology, illustrating how the core mechanisms operate in practical systems. We analyze standard examples, clarify potential pitfalls, and discuss optimization considerations. By the end of this lecture, you will have a thorough mastery of Phases of a compiler and translation passes ready for assignments and exams.','2026-09-10 08:37:50','2026-09-10 08:37:50'),(139,208,'Welcome to this focused lecture on Role of lexical analyzer and regular expressions, part of the Introduction to Compilers curriculum in Compiler Design. In today\'s session, we break down Role of lexical analyzer and regular expressions step-by-step. We begin by examining the underlying motivation and academic significance of this topic. Next, we trace the formal methodology, illustrating how the core mechanisms operate in practical systems. We analyze standard examples, clarify potential pitfalls, and discuss optimization considerations. By the end of this lecture, you will have a thorough mastery of Role of lexical analyzer and regular expressions ready for assignments and exams.','2026-09-10 08:37:50','2026-09-10 08:37:50'),(140,209,'Welcome to this focused lecture on Finite automata and Thompson construction, part of the Introduction to Compilers curriculum in Compiler Design. In today\'s session, we break down Finite automata and Thompson construction step-by-step. We begin by examining the underlying motivation and academic significance of this topic. Next, we trace the formal methodology, illustrating how the core mechanisms operate in practical systems. We analyze standard examples, clarify potential pitfalls, and discuss optimization considerations. By the end of this lecture, you will have a thorough mastery of Finite automata and Thompson construction ready for assignments and exams.','2026-09-10 08:37:51','2026-09-10 08:37:51'),(141,210,'Welcome to this focused lecture on Bootstrapping and LEX lexical analyzer generator, part of the Introduction to Compilers curriculum in Compiler Design. In today\'s session, we break down Bootstrapping and LEX lexical analyzer generator step-by-step. We begin by examining the underlying motivation and academic significance of this topic. Next, we trace the formal methodology, illustrating how the core